# Testing Methodology

## 1. Lab Identification
The assessment was performed in an intentionally vulnerable PortSwigger Web Security Academy laboratory.

## 2. Request Analysis
Burp Suite was used to intercept and analyse the application's stock-check HTTP request.

## 3. Parameter Testing
The `storeId` parameter was tested to determine whether user-controlled input could influence the underlying SQL query.

## 4. SQL Injection Validation
SQL expressions were submitted and the application's responses were compared to determine whether SQL Injection was present.

## 5. Filter Analysis
A UNION-based SQL Injection test resulted in an "Attack detected" response, indicating that input filtering was detecting the attempted payload.

## 6. Filter Bypass Investigation
XML entity encoding was investigated as part of the controlled laboratory exercise to understand how input filtering could be bypassed.

## 7. Evidence Collection
Screenshots were captured throughout the assessment to document requests, responses, testing results, and the completed laboratory objective.

## 8. Impact Assessment
The potential security impact of the identified vulnerability was assessed based on what an attacker could achieve if the weakness existed in a real application.

## 9. Remediation
Recommendations were documented to help prevent SQL Injection, including parameterized queries/prepared statements and appropriate input validation.
